            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Victory, righteous judgment, and conquest, most prominently symbolizing Jesus Christ's victorious return to defeat His enemies and establish His kingdom.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Revelation 19:11-14\",\"Revelation 6:2\",\"Zechariah 1:8\",\"Zechariah 6:3-6\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The Antichrist, false peace, or deceptive conquest (specifically regarding the rider of the first seal in Revelation 6).\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 6:2\",\"Matthew 24:4-5\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Human military might, worldly power, and the reliance on physical strength rather than God (referring to 'horse' in general).\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 20:7\",\"Isaiah 31:1-3\",\"Deuteronomy 17:16\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"The victorious and unstoppable spread of the Gospel throughout the world (an alternative view of the first seal).\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 6:2\",\"Psalm 45:4-5\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the white horse in Revelation 19 is universally accepted as symbolizing Christ's righteous victory, there is a major, long-standing debate regarding the white horse of Revelation 6 (the first seal). Many view the Revelation 6 rider as the Antichrist or false conquest, whereas others see it as Christ or the Gospel. Additionally, the 'horse' in general biblical symbolism represents warfare and military might.\"}",
